#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176837} :activity/id #uuid "b9035507-8754-5ae2-a1ef-580b9406be50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049746 :topic/id #uuid "109cf37d-ad54-5b53-ba64-1f478bc5ca24" :topic/math-academy-id 3664} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176014 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175867 :question/id #uuid "d3520d29-1ecb-5bb0-9c1c-c530fa681853" :question/math-academy-id "e-9316"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31bf2725-d2ba-53a8-bbdf-0fd7cd643aca" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500331}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling with Inverse Square Laws"} :step/id #uuid "d5420dd5-0691-5226-8163-3d5f81ffc20f" :step/math-academy-id 34865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176787 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176015 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175868 :question/id #uuid "c5c908e5-aa2b-5ba3-bf6c-dfa6fc49c386" :question/math-academy-id "e-9318"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "920b0639-5f8d-5b4a-b688-4326d14c2a86" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049369}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500337}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Constant of Variation"} :step/id #uuid "9bec95a6-c4cf-5bb2-9b8d-d3c10163aa97" :step/math-academy-id 34868 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176917}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176791 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177027 :tutorial/content "![](images/a2/a2bbedb8a024b6cd75c333e2c0cf025558b71eafeb7215d9c7dbf2fbd678230a.png)\n\nIt is known that the gravitational force $F,$ measured in newtons, that the earth exerts on the moon varies inversely with the **square** of the distance $r,$ in kilometers, between their centers.\n\nAccording to this information, the equation that relates $F$ and $r$ is\n\n$$\nF = \\dfrac{k}{r^2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of variation. This type of relationship is called an **inverse square law**.\n\nAs it turns out, many important physical laws take the form of an inverse square law. Let's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "6e6cc0ce-3bd6-5947-b049-60a63924bd23" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5696 :tutorial/title "Inverse Square Laws"} :step/id #uuid "3b1ff76e-dab3-5822-9f2b-e01a3ba037bd" :step/math-academy-id 34964 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176786}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176961 :tutorial/content "When an object moves at a constant speed $v,$ the distance $d$ that it travels and the time $t$ are related by the equation\n\n$$\nd = vt.\n$$\n\nSuppose that we want to know how long it takes for an object to travel a *fixed* distance. We start by rewriting the above equation for $t\\mathbin :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{d}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $d$ is fixed (i.e. constant), it follows that the time $t$ varies inversely with the velocity $v,$ and $d$ is the constant of variation. \n\nIntuitively, the fact that this is an inverse variation makes sense. The larger the speed of our object, the smaller the time it takes to cover a fixed distance, and vice-versa.\n\nSuppose we're told that an object takes $t=10$ seconds to cover a particular distance when traveling with a speed of $v=5$ meters per second. Then, we can solve for $d$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{d}{v} \\\\ 10 & =\\frac{d}{5} \\\\ 10\\cdot 5 & =d \\\\ 50 & =d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the inverse variation equation\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{50}{v}.\n$$\n\nWe can now use this equation to find the amount of time the object takes to cover the same fixed distance for different speeds." :tutorial/id #uuid "d738193b-1de7-58d9-b145-0f84d66d9c3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1512 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7f194aeb-30f4-5e7e-8022-d36f14b0c127" :step/math-academy-id 3737 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176787}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176078 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175745 :question/id #uuid "231b7b37-4fdc-5535-a73f-7fb743091311" :question/math-academy-id "e-1581"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91394e22-dc2a-5304-b9aa-80302de38fb1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500361}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling With Inverse Variation"} :step/id #uuid "14b0e70f-af6c-594e-b86d-ef230d231ad1" :step/math-academy-id 4995 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176791}}] :activity/title "Modeling With Inverse Variation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176168}]}
